@@ -2,6 +2,52 @@
 
 This project is a sample news application built with Astro, designed to serve as a testing ground for new features in Chrome DevTools.
 
+## Accounts and passkeys
+
+Sign up at `/devtools-times/signup` with a username and password, then optionally
+create a passkey. Sign-in supports both an explicit passkey button and passkeys in
+username autofill, alongside password sign-in. After signing in, **Account settings**
+in the header opens `/devtools-times/account/settings` to create, rename, or remove
+passkeys and update the account profile. Registration in settings supports security
+keys as well as platform authenticators; the post-signup offer uses the platform
+authenticator. Canceling a browser prompt leaves the password flow available.
+
+Profile and passkey changes require an authentication within the last five minutes.
+When necessary, the UI asks for a passkey belonging to the active account (with user
+verification required) or its password, then resumes the requested change. Removing
+the last passkey is safe because every account retains a password. Supported browsers
+also receive best-effort updates when credentials are removed or account names change.
+
+The forms now use the same-origin `api/auth/*` endpoints. Accounts created through
+the previous external sample password API are separate and are not migrated; create
+an account here to use these flows. Passwords are salted and scrypt-hashed, public
+keys and counters are stored on the server, and sessions use HttpOnly, SameSite cookies.
+WebAuthn signatures, origin, RP ID, presence, account ownership, and single-use,
+five-minute session-bound challenges are checked with
+[@simplewebauthn/server](https://simplewebauthn.dev/docs/packages/server).
+
+Passkeys require HTTPS in deployment, or `http://localhost` locally. Use
+`.env.example` as a reference and supply runtime environment variables:
+`WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN` is the exact public origin and `WEBAUTHN_RP_ID` is the stable
+relying-party domain. The standalone Node adapter does not load `.env` automatically;
+inject these variables through the deployment environment. On localhost, leave these two
+variables unset to use the local origin and domain. The server returns its RP ID;
+the browser never guesses it. Keep that RP ID stable to retain access to existing
+passkeys. Browser features are detected, JSON/capability methods use
+`webauthn-polyfills`, and unsupported browsers retain password access.
+
+`AUTH_STORE_PATH` defaults to `.data/auth.json`, an ignored, atomic JSON store for
+this single-instance sample. Mount persistent storage to retain accounts across
+deployments. Before running multiple instances, replace it with a shared transactional
+database and shared rate limiting; the file transaction queue and request rate limiter
+are process-local. Provider labels/icons use a small checked-in subset of the
+[passkey AAGUID registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids);
+unknown providers use a generic icon and are fully supported. AAGUIDs affect display only.
+
+`pnpm run build` builds without starting the application. `pnpm test` checks password
+hashing, identifier encoding, persistent store serialization, and expiry cleanup without
+starting a server or importing a server entrypoint.
+
 
 ```sh
 pnpm install && pnpm run start
